  "result": "It's fixed: the command now prints **27.06** (it was printing 26.09).\n\n**Root cause:** In `shop/money.py:9`, `to_cents` turns a price string into whole cents. It padded the decimal part on the left (`frac.rjust(2, \"0\")`), so `\"12.5\"` became `\"12.05\"` and was read as 1205 cents instead of 1250. The fix is one change: pad on the right with `frac.ljust(2, \"0\")`.\n\nWith the correct price, two items cost 2500 cents, and 8.25% tax adds 206.25, rounded to 206, for a total of 27.06.\n\n**Tests:** pytest isn't installed, so I ran them with `python3 -m unittest`, and all 6 pass. I also spot-checked other inputs: `12` \u2192 1200, `12.5` \u2192 1250, `12.05` \u2192 1205, `12.50` \u2192 1250, `0.5` \u2192 50. None of the existing tests covers a price with a single decimal digit, so adding one for `to_cents(\"12.5\")` would stop this from coming back.",
